"""Core bilingual lessons, keyed by language and week."""

EN = [
("Does my loop fit in 1 ms?", "Linear search and a control-loop budget",
 "Our mobile robot reads a batch of range sensors at 1 kHz. The cycle is 1 ms. Reading sensors and updating the motors already take 0.7 ms, leaving 0.3 ms to find the first unsafe reading.",
 "[72, 48, 19, 12] cm. Stop at the first reading below 20 cm. Write the position, reading, comparison and decision for each check. Repeat with a threshold of 10 cm.",
 "At 20 cm, the third reading is the first unsafe one, after three checks. At 10 cm the scan uses four checks and reports no match. With n readings the worst case uses n checks. Estimate time using the measured scan and keep a margin for variation.",
 "Predict which takes longer, first hit or no hit. Run with 100 and 1,000 readings. Record the longest observed batch-average and compare with the 0.3 ms allocation. Describe how you would check the deadline on the robot.",
 [
 ("What period corresponds to 1 kHz?", "1/1000 s = 1 ms."),
 ("If other tasks take 0.7 ms, what is left?", "0.3 ms."),
 ("Trace [40, 20, 18] at threshold 20 with a strict comparison.", "Check all three. Position 3 is the first unsafe reading."),
 ("If the first reading is unsafe, how many checks?", "One."),
 ("If there are no readings, what should this search report?", "No match, after zero checks. Sensor failure handling is a separate system decision."),
 ("A scan of 100 readings takes 0.04 ms. Estimate 1,000 at the same rate.", "0.4 ms. That estimate exceeds the 0.3 ms allocation."),
 ("What input should you include to measure a long scan?", "A batch with no unsafe reading or one with the only hit at the end."),
 ("Does a 0.22 ms laptop measurement establish the robot deadline?", "No. Measure the complete cycle on the target under representative load and check the margin."),
 ]),
("What happens when n grows 10×?", "Growth shapes, counts and timing",
 "The robot gets a denser range scan. A single pass checks each point. A divide-and-merge pass does about n log₂ n work, while a comparison of every ordered pair does n². Which is likely to grow beyond the budget first?",
 "Fill the table for n = 10, 100 and 1,000. Count n, approximate n log₂ n and count n². Then trace one four-point scan, a four-point merge tree with two levels, and the 4 × 4 table of ordered pairs including self-pairs.",
 "The four-point scan has 4 visits. The merge tree handles 4 items at each of 2 levels, or 8 item visits in the simplified model. The pair table has 16 entries. Big-O names how the work grows as input gets large. The units, constants and machine still matter for timing.",
 "Predict the ratios before running. Compare scans, merge sort and pair loops at n = 100 and 1,000. Compare ratios within each method. Explain why Python timings need not match the operation-count ratios.",
 [
 ("How many ordered comparisons for n = 100, including self-pairs?", "10,000."),
 ("What happens to n² when n grows by 10×?", "It grows by 100×."),
 ("What happens to n in the same change?", "It grows by 10×."),
 ("For powers of two, which has more merge levels, 64 or 128?", "128 has 7 levels, one more than 64."),
 ("A scan measures 0.08 ms at n = 100. Estimate at n = 1,000.", "About 0.8 ms if the measured rate holds."),
 ("A pair loop measures 0.08 ms at n = 100. Estimate at n = 1,000.", "About 8 ms under the same assumption."),
 ("How large is an n × n table of one-byte entries at n = 1,000?", "1,000,000 bytes before representation overhead."),
 ("Two methods cross in your measurements. Which should you use at a fixed operating size?", "Compare measured time and memory at that size, with headroom and relevant worst inputs."),
 ]),
("How do I read cost from code?", "Loop bounds, halving and binary search",
 "The robot converts thermistor ADC readings using a calibration table sorted by ADC value. Find the insertion index for a reading, then inspect the adjacent calibration entries if interpolation is needed.",
 "Trace a binary search for 430 in [120, 220, 330, 430, 550, 680, 790, 900]. Use zero-based indices and a half-open interval [lo, hi). Inspect the middle. If its value is smaller than the target, set lo = mid + 1. Otherwise set hi = mid. Stop at lo = hi.",
 "The inspected indices are 4, 2, 3 and the insertion index is 3. Each comparison discards roughly half the remaining candidate elements. A loop that scans n rows and m columns uses n × m visits. Two successive scans use n + m.",
 "Predict how scan and binary lookup behave as the sorted calibration table grows. Time both with the same above-maximum target, then try a first-entry hit. Keep table construction outside the measurement.",
 [
 ("Trace the insertion index for 500 in the table.", "Index 4, between 430 and 550."),
 ("Where does a target above 900 go?", "Index 8, after the last entry. Handle that boundary before interpolating."),
 ("What index does an empty table return?", "0. There is no calibration pair to interpolate."),
 ("A loop halves 64 until it reaches 1. How many halvings?", "Six."),
 ("How many visits for 12 rows and 8 columns?", "96."),
 ("How many visits in successive scans of 12 and 8 readings?", "20."),
 ("Will this binary search work on unsorted ADC values?", "The result is unreliable because the discarded half can contain the correct position."),
 ("When might a scan be a better fit?", "For a tiny unsorted table queried once, sorting and maintaining order may cost more than scanning."),
 ]),
("How do I store a sensor stream?", "Arrays, linked lists, ring buffers and stacks",
 "A sensor produces data faster than the robot can sometimes consume it. Use a fixed-capacity FIFO to keep memory bounded. Choose a policy for overflow. Here the oldest sample is overwritten and a loss counter is incremented.",
 "Draw three slots. Push A, B and C, pop once, then push D and E. Record head, size, and the logical FIFO order after each action. Separately push A and B onto a stack and pop once.",
 "The FIFO pop returns A. D wraps into the freed slot. E overwrites B. The final FIFO is C, D, E and one sample was lost. The stack returns B. A ring uses index arithmetic on an array. A linked queue can also enqueue and dequeue in constant work with head and tail pointers, but uses links and often allocates nodes.",
 "Predict which grows with buffer size, shifting an array on every pop or advancing a ring index. Time repeated full-buffer push/pop cycles. Compare at both capacities and account for bytes per sample separately.",
 [
 ("What does FIFO mean for timestamp order?", "The oldest queued sample comes out first."),
 ("What does a stack pop after pushes 5, 9?", "9."),
 ("What slot follows index 3 in a four-slot ring?", "Index 0."),
 ("How many samples remain after pushing four into an empty capacity-three overwrite ring?", "Three. One was lost."),
 ("How much payload fits in 128 slots of 8 bytes?", "1,024 bytes, plus indices and any representation overhead."),
 ("Why track size or a full flag?", "Equal head and tail indices can represent either a full or an empty buffer."),
 ("Which representation supports direct access by index?", "An array gives direct indexing. A linked list generally follows links."),
 ("If every sample must be retained, is overwrite a fit?", "No. Reduce the production rate, increase throughput, or provide capacity for the worst expected burst."),
 ]),
("How do I look things up fast?", "Sorted tables and hash lookup",
 "The robot gets a fault code and must retrieve its response. A static sorted table supports exact lookup and ordered queries. A hash table is convenient for exact keys when memory and lookup variation are acceptable.",
 "Insert codes 7, 12 and 17 into five empty slots using code mod 5 and linear probing. Then look up 17 and the absent code 22. Compare with binary search in the sorted table [7, 12, 17].",
 "All three keys start at slot 2. They occupy slots 2, 3 and 4. A lookup for 17 probes three slots. A lookup for 22 continues to empty slot 0, after four probes. This example has no deletion. A hash table needs spare slots and collisions can increase work.",
 "Predict which method will be quicker for repeated exact queries. Time a binary lookup and a Python dictionary lookup at both sizes. Prepare the structures before timing. Compare memory separately if choosing for a small controller.",
 [
 ("What is 17 mod 5?", "2."),
 ("Why do 7 and 12 collide?", "Both produce starting slot 2."),
 ("Which slot is inspected after slot 4 in this table?", "Slot 0."),
 ("What key does exact lookup of 13 in [7, 12, 17] return?", "No match. Its insertion index alone is insufficient."),
 ("Which structure gives nearby smaller and larger fault codes naturally?", "A sorted table."),
 ("What happens when many keys hash to the same slot?", "Probe sequences get longer."),
 ("Does a single fast dictionary lookup guarantee a tight deadline?", "No. Consider collisions, possible resizing, and the target implementation."),
 ("How would you compare methods for 1,000 repeated queries?", "Use the same keys and hit/miss mix, and report build time and storage separately."),
 ]),
("How do I filter noisy data?", "Median windows and sorting choices",
 "One range reading spikes to 200 cm near a wall. A median filter uses the middle of a small window to reduce the influence of that outlier.",
 "Insertion-sort [12, 200, 11, 13, 10] on paper. After each insertion write the sorted prefix. Read the median. Then merge [10, 13] with [11, 12, 200].",
 "The prefixes grow to [12, 200], [11, 12, 200], [11, 12, 13, 200], then [10, 11, 12, 13, 200]. The median is 12 cm. Merge sort combines sorted halves and commonly needs temporary space. Quicksort partitions around a pivot. Poor pivots can give quadratic work.",
 "Compare the insertion-sort median with Python’s sorted-based median for odd windows 5 and 31. Use the same unsorted input and include the copy needed to keep the sensor window intact.",
 [
 ("What is the median of [3, 90, 5]?", "5."),
 ("Insert 4 into the sorted prefix [2, 6, 8].", "[2, 4, 6, 8]. Shift 8 and 6."),
 ("Merge [1, 7] and [2, 4].", "[1, 2, 4, 7]."),
 ("What happens when insertion sort receives an already sorted window?", "Each item stays in place, with roughly one comparison per insertion."),
 ("What is the median of five identical readings?", "That same reading."),
 ("Why keep an odd window for this activity?", "There is a single middle position."),
 ("How can bad quicksort pivots cost more?", "Each partition can leave almost all items for the next partition."),
 ("Why might a larger median window be undesirable?", "It adds computation and can delay the response to a real change."),
 ]),
("Which task runs next?", "Priority queues and timer heaps",
 "The robot scheduler stores future release times for diagnostics and telemetry. A min-heap helps it find the next timer to release. Once a timer is due, the scheduler still needs a policy for running the released tasks.",
 "Start with heap [4, 9, 7]. Insert deadline 3, then remove the minimum. Use zero-based child indices 2i + 1 and 2i + 2. Break equal deadlines by insertion order.",
 "Append 3 and swap upward past 9 and 4 to get [3, 4, 7, 9]. Remove 3, move 9 to the root and swap down with 4 to get [4, 9, 7]. Peeking reads one entry. An insertion or removal moves along at most one tree path, so work grows roughly with log₂ n.",
 "Predict how selecting and replacing one timer scales in an unsorted list and a heap. Time one replacement per call at two queue sizes. Keep initial heap construction outside the timing.",
 [
 ("What is the next deadline in [4, 9, 7]?", "4."),
 ("Which child should replace a larger root during a sift-down?", "The smaller child, with the chosen tie rule."),
 ("What parent index belongs to child index 5?", "Index 2."),
 ("Is the whole array [4, 9, 7] sorted?", "No. Each parent is at most its children."),
 ("What should the scheduler do if its heap is empty?", "Report no pending timer and avoid popping."),
 ("Two timers have the same timestamp. How can the order be deterministic?", "Use a sequence number after the deadline in the comparison."),
 ("What does a heap tell you when the earliest timer is later than now?", "No pending timer is due yet."),
 ("If removing the next timer takes 0.03 ms and your dispatcher has 0.02 ms left, does it fit?", "The observed operation exceeds that allocation."),
 ]),
]

